children.translated_text:
The resounding crash of the axe raised to break the bow-staff adorned with the crescent moon [held] by his staff-like arm;
the kettledrum-herald of the prelude of the Noble One's childhood exploits;
the fierce whirling within the shell of the cosmic egg quickly overturned and covering the skull-bowl —
how, alas, does it not yet rest?

In this village, O traveler, no lodging is given to wayfarers now;
at night, a young traveler slept in the hall of the pleasure-pavilion;
therefore, rising up, that rogue cries out when the clouds thicken, remembering his beloved — because of that,
even today people stand in fear of the falling of skull-topped staffs.

You are thus [endowed] with beauty, and he is familiar with loveliness;
you two alone reach the limit of the arts.
Ah, fortunate pair! — thus, O fair one, the left [half] speaks;
if the rest could be, then that would be conquered by the accomplished one.

Dark as the petals of the blue lotus, the clouds have departed from the sky;
dark as the petals of the blue lotus, <s>Keśava</s> (<s>Kṛṣṇa</s>) awakens from sleep;
dark as the petals of the blue lotus, the dark <s>Śyāmā</s> creeper blossoms today;
dark as the petals of the blue lotus, the moon pervades the sky.
